1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.269
Welcome to Building Coding Agents with Tool Execution,

2
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.572
both in partnership with E2B

3
00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:07.874
and taught by Tereza Tizkova

4
00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:09.776
and Francesco Zuppichinni.

5
00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:12.145
In this course, you learn how to

6
00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:14.447
build agents that can generate and execute code

7
00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:16.750
as part of how they get the work done.

8
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.252
Many of us will predefine some set of functions

9
00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.454
that we give to, say, an LLM to call.

10
00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:24.691
But rather than using only a predefined

11
00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:26.693
small set of functions,

12
00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:29.095
if you instead let your LLM or your agent

13
00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:31.798
write and execute arbitrary Python code.

14
00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:35.602
then has access to every built-in Python function

15
00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:38.038
or can even ask to install common

16
00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:40.173
Python packages and call functions from that.

17
00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:41.975
This really opens up the space

18
00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:43.676
of what an agent can now do.

19
00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:47.680
Also, instead of sequentially making function calls one at a time,

20
00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:52.185
which is common in LLM tool use for slightly more complex tasks,

21
00:00:52.285 --> 00:00:55.588
your agent can now also just write a huge chunk of code.

22
00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:59.626
and efficiently execute a long sequence of operations,

23
00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:02.529
and this too makes it more capable.

24
00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:05.832
But running code generated by an LLM

25
00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:08.435
on your local machine can be unsafe.

26
00:01:08.635 --> 00:01:11.738
What if the code does something it isn't supposed to?

27
00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:15.141
Once, a highly agentic coding tool

28
00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.244
deleted one of my team members' important project files.

29
00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:23.516
And another did a flawed database migration that deleted production data.

30
00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.318
Thankfully, we had backups.

31
00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:28.521
But to run generated code safely,

32
00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:32.125
a good practice is to run that code inside a sandbox environment

33
00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:35.428
so that the code execution is isolated from your system.

34
00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:38.164
Thanks Andrew. In this course,

35
00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:39.766
we will start by breaking down

36
00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:41.634
what an AI coding agent really is

37
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.937
and how it differs from other AI agents.

38
00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:47.140
You will learn how an LLM can reason step-by-step,

39
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.442
decide which tool to call, execute code,

40
00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:51.845
and use the result to plan its next action.

41
00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:55.849
And then, you'll build your own agent capable of running Python code,

42
00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:58.651
reading and writing files and iterating through tasks

43
00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:00.453
until it reaches a final answer.

44
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:05.058
We'll discuss how to manage the context window, handle errors, and retries.

45
00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:08.194
Once you understand how an agent thinks and acts,

46
00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:11.231
you'll explore different environments like local execution,

47
00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:14.200
containers, and secure sandboxed microVMs.

48
00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:16.703
And you will learn how to choose the right one.

49
00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:18.805
depending on your use case.

50
00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:21.908
You'll learn how to create and manage sandboxes, run code remotely

51
00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:25.512
in different languages and interact with the sandbox file system.

52
00:02:25.512 --> 00:02:28.715
So your agent will be able to use uploaded dataset,

53
00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:32.485
save outputs and even create and host small web application.

54
00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:34.888
You build a data analyst agent

55
00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:37.290
that explores CSV files with pandas

56
00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.292
and generates clear visualization.

57
00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:42.695
And in a final lesson, we'll create a full-stack agent

58
00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:45.565
that edits multiple files and builds a working

59
00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:48.368
Next.JS web app inside the sandbox.

60
00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:52.172
To keep your agent focused even across large, multifile projects,

61
00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:56.142
we'd also discuss managing long context through runtime summarization.

62
00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.645
Many people have worked to create this course.

63
00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.915
I'd like to thank Jakub Dobry from E2B

64
00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:04.551
and from DeepLearning.AI, Esmaeil Gargari

65
00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:06.553
also contributed to this course.

66
00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:08.488
The first lesson will introduce you

67
00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.490
to the inner workings of coding agents,

68
00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.892
as well as some challenges in building them.

69
00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.829
So, let's go on to the next video and get started.
